  "notes": [
    "Supply: 3.0-3.6 V able to deliver 500 mA (Wi-Fi TX peaks); 22 uF (10 uF minimum) and 100 nF at the 3V3 pin.",
    "Strapping pins sampled at reset: IO0 (weak pull-up; low = download mode), IO46 (weak pull-down; keep low/floating), IO45 (weak pull-down; must stay low for the 3.3 V flash), IO3 (JTAG source). Do not load them with circuits that pull the wrong way during reset.",
    "N4R8/N8R8/N16R8 (octal PSRAM): IO35, IO36, IO37 are taken by the PSRAM; do not use them. R16V variants: IO47/IO48 are 1.8 V I/O.",
    "Every free GPIO reaches UART, SPI, I2C, I2S, LEDC PWM, MCPWM, TWAI (CAN; needs an external transceiver), RMT and PCNT through the GPIO matrix; ADC2 is not usable while Wi-Fi runs, use ADC1 (IO1-IO10) for analog.",
    "Antenna: the antenna area is the 6 mm at the top edge of the module (opposite the bottom pin row); no metal enclosure near it. The footprint keeps tracks, vias, pours and other parts out of the antenna area itself (the module's width plus 0.5 mm, and 0.5 mm beyond its edge), the minimum the datasheet marks as the keepout zone. The vendor's hardware design guidelines (Positioning a Module on a Base Board) strongly recommend the antenna outside the base board, or its feed point at the board edge with the board cut away beside and below it, and at least 15 mm of clearance around it in the product: place the module at a board edge with the antenna overhanging it (`place = [edge(.top, overhang = 6mm)]`: the rule's `overhang` is a Length, how far beyond the edge), keep copper and parts as far from the antenna as the board allows, and put ground copper with dense ground vias on the board near (not under) the antenna.",
    "The EPAD (pin 41) to ground with thermal vias improves heat flow; use modest solder paste on it so the module does not float."
  ],
